.TH "vsearch-sam" "5" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch file formats"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
sam \[em] a text-based format for representing sequence alignments
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
SAM (Sequence Alignment/Map) is a tab-delimited text format for storing
the alignment of one or more \f[I]query\f[R] sequences against one or
more \f[I]reference\f[R] sequences.
It was designed by the 1000 Genomes Project and is maintained by the
hts-specs working group:
.PP
<https://github.com/samtools/hts-specs>
.PP
The format is defined in the \f[I]SAMv1\f[R] specification, and optional
alignment tags are listed in the \f[I]SAMtags\f[R] specification.
vsearch produces SAM version 1.0 and writes each record on a single line
with fields separated by a single horizontal tab character (ASCII 9).
Lines are terminated by a single line feed character (ASCII 10).
.PP
A SAM file produced by vsearch consists of an optional \f[I]header\f[R]
followed by zero or more \f[I]alignment records\f[R].
The \f[V]--samout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R] option selects SAM as the
output format for the \f[V]--allpairs_global\f[R],
\f[V]--cluster_fast\f[R], \f[V]--cluster_size\f[R],
\f[V]--cluster_smallmem\f[R], \f[V]--cluster_unoise\f[R],
\f[V]--search_exact\f[R], and \f[V]--usearch_global\f[R] commands.
The \f[V]--samheader\f[R] option requests that the optional header be
written; by default no header is produced.
.PP
vsearch does not read SAM files and does not produce the binary BAM or
CRAM formats.
Conversion between SAM, BAM, and CRAM can be performed with external
tools such as \f[V]samtools(1)\f[R].
.SS Header
.PP
When \f[V]--samheader\f[R] is given, vsearch writes a header consisting
of one \f[V]\[at]HD\f[R] line, one \f[V]\[at]SQ\f[R] line per reference
sequence, and one \f[V]\[at]PG\f[R] line, in that order.
All header lines start with the character \f[V]\[at]\f[R] immediately
followed by a two-letter record type code, and contain tab-separated
\f[V]TAG:value\f[R] fields:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]HD VN:1.0  SO:unsorted GO:query
\[at]SQ SN:name LN:length   M5:md5  UR:file:dbname
\[at]PG ID:vsearch  VN:version  CL:command-line
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[V]\[at]HD\f[R]
File-level metadata.
\f[V]VN\f[R] is the SAM format version (\f[V]1.0\f[R]).
\f[V]SO\f[R] is the sort order of alignment records
(\f[V]unsorted\f[R]).
\f[V]GO\f[R] is the grouping of alignment records (\f[V]query\f[R]:
records for the same query are consecutive).
.TP
\f[V]\[at]SQ\f[R]
Reference sequence dictionary.
One \f[V]\[at]SQ\f[R] line is emitted per sequence in the reference
database, in database order.
\f[V]SN\f[R] is the reference name, taken from the fasta header up to
the first white space (or the entire header line with
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]).
\f[V]LN\f[R] is the reference length in residues.
\f[V]M5\f[R] is the MD5 digest of the reference sequence, as 32
lowercase hexadecimal digits.
\f[V]UR\f[R] is the location of the reference, written as
\f[V]file:\f[R] followed by the database filename as given on the
command line.
.TP
\f[V]\[at]PG\f[R]
Program record.
\f[V]ID\f[R] is the program name (\f[V]vsearch\f[R]).
\f[V]VN\f[R] is the vsearch version.
\f[V]CL\f[R] is the full command line used to invoke vsearch.
.PP
vsearch does not emit \f[V]\[at]RG\f[R] (read group) or
\f[V]\[at]CO\f[R] (comment) header lines.
.SS Alignment records
.PP
Each alignment record occupies a single line with eleven mandatory
tab-separated fields followed by zero or more optional tags:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
QNAME   FLAG    RNAME   POS MAPQ    CIGAR   RNEXT   PNEXT   TLEN    SEQ QUAL    [TAG:TYPE:VALUE ...]
\f[R]
.fi
.IP " 1." 4
\f[V]QNAME\f[R] \[em] query template name.
The query label as it appears in the input fasta or fastq file.
.IP " 2." 4
\f[V]FLAG\f[R] \[em] bitwise flag, written as a decimal integer.
vsearch only sets the following bits:
.RS 4
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]0x004\f[R] (\f[V]4\f[R]): the query is unmapped (no alignment was
produced).
Set when the query has no hit and \f[V]--output_no_hits\f[R] is in
effect.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]0x010\f[R] (\f[V]16\f[R]): the query aligned on the reverse strand
of the reference.
Set for reverse-strand hits produced with \f[V]--strand   both\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]0x100\f[R] (\f[V]256\f[R]): the record is a secondary alignment.
Set for the second and following hits reported for a given query when
\f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R] and \f[V]--top_hits_only\f[R] allow multiple
hits.
All other bits are always \f[V]0\f[R]; in particular, vsearch never sets
the paired-end bits (\f[V]0x001\f[R], \f[V]0x002\f[R], \f[V]0x040\f[R],
\f[V]0x080\f[R]), the mate bits (\f[V]0x008\f[R], \f[V]0x020\f[R]), the
quality-control bit (\f[V]0x200\f[R]), the duplicate bit
(\f[V]0x400\f[R]), or the supplementary bit (\f[V]0x800\f[R]).
.RE
.IP " 3." 4
\f[V]RNAME\f[R] \[em] reference sequence name.
The target label as it appears in the reference database, matching the
\f[V]SN\f[R] value of the corresponding \f[V]\[at]SQ\f[R] header line.
\f[V]*\f[R] for unmapped queries.
.IP " 4." 4
\f[V]POS\f[R] \[em] 1-based leftmost mapping position of the query on
the reference.
Always \f[V]1\f[R] for mapped queries (vsearch performs global alignment
and reports the alignment relative to the start of the reference).
\f[V]0\f[R] for unmapped queries.
.IP " 5." 4
\f[V]MAPQ\f[R] \[em] mapping quality.
Always \f[V]255\f[R], the value defined by the SAM specification to mean
\f[I]mapping quality not available\f[R].
.IP " 6." 4
\f[V]CIGAR\f[R] \[em] alignment operations in CIGAR format, with the
operation letters \f[V]M\f[R] (match or mismatch), \f[V]I\f[R]
(insertion in the query relative to the reference), and \f[V]D\f[R]
(deletion in the query relative to the reference).
vsearch always emits explicit run-lengths and does not use the
\f[V]=\f[R] and \f[V]X\f[R] operations.
\f[V]*\f[R] for unmapped queries.
See \f[V]vsearch-cigar(5)\f[R].
.IP " 7." 4
\f[V]RNEXT\f[R] \[em] reference sequence name of the mate.
Always \f[V]*\f[R] (vsearch does not output paired-end information).
.IP " 8." 4
\f[V]PNEXT\f[R] \[em] position of the mate.
Always \f[V]0\f[R].
.IP " 9." 4
\f[V]TLEN\f[R] \[em] observed template length.
Always \f[V]0\f[R].
.IP "10." 4
\f[V]SEQ\f[R] \[em] query segment.
For forward-strand hits, the original query sequence.
For reverse-strand hits (flag bit \f[V]0x010\f[R] set), the reverse
complement of the original query sequence.
For unmapped queries, the original query sequence.
vsearch never uses \f[V]*\f[R] in this field.
.IP "11." 4
\f[V]QUAL\f[R] \[em] ASCII-encoded Phred quality scores (base 33) for
each residue in \f[V]SEQ\f[R].
Always \f[V]*\f[R] (not available), even when the input is a fastq file.
.PP
For each mapped query, vsearch emits one alignment record per reported
hit.
The number and ordering of hits are controlled by
\f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R], \f[V]--maxrejects\f[R],
\f[V]--top_hits_only\f[R], and other search-related options.
The first record of a group has flag bit \f[V]0x100\f[R] cleared and
represents the primary alignment; subsequent records for the same query
have flag bit \f[V]0x100\f[R] set and represent secondary alignments.
.SS Optional tags
.PP
After the eleven mandatory fields, vsearch appends the following
optional tags, in order, for each mapped query.
Each tag is written as \f[V]TAG:TYPE:VALUE\f[R] and separated from the
previous field by a tab.
Unmapped queries carry no optional tags.
.TP
\f[V]AS:i:\f[R]\f[I]integer\f[R]
Alignment score.
vsearch stores the percent identity of the alignment, rounded to the
nearest integer.
This differs from the SAMtags recommendation, which describes
\f[V]AS\f[R] as an aligner-specific alignment score; vsearch follows the
convention established by usearch.
.TP
\f[V]XN:i:0\f[R]
Next best alignment score.
Always \f[V]0\f[R] (not computed by vsearch).
.TP
\f[V]XM:i:\f[R]\f[I]integer\f[R]
Number of mismatches in the alignment, excluding terminal gaps.
.TP
\f[V]XO:i:\f[R]\f[I]integer\f[R]
Number of gap opens in the alignment, excluding terminal gaps.
.TP
\f[V]XG:i:\f[R]\f[I]integer\f[R]
Total number of gap extensions in the alignment, excluding terminal
gaps.
Equivalent to the total length of internal gaps.
.TP
\f[V]NM:i:\f[R]\f[I]integer\f[R]
Edit distance to the reference, defined as the sum of \f[V]XM\f[R] and
\f[V]XG\f[R] (mismatches plus total internal gap length).
.TP
\f[V]MD:Z:\f[R]\f[I]string\f[R]
Mismatching positions and deleted reference bases, encoded as described
in the SAMtags specification.
The \f[V]MD\f[R] string allows the reference sequence within the aligned
region to be reconstructed from \f[V]SEQ\f[R] and \f[V]CIGAR\f[R].
It consists of a sequence of decimal numbers (counts of consecutive
matching bases), uppercase letters (mismatched reference bases), and
\f[V]\[ha]\f[R]-prefixed uppercase letter runs (reference bases deleted
from the query).
Insertions in the query are not represented in \f[V]MD\f[R]; they are
recorded only in \f[V]CIGAR\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]YT:Z:UU\f[R]
Alignment type in bowtie2 notation.
Always \f[V]UU\f[R] (unpaired read aligned to a single unpaired
reference).
vsearch does not align paired reads.
.PP
The \f[V]XN\f[R], \f[V]XM\f[R], \f[V]XO\f[R], \f[V]XG\f[R],
\f[V]MD\f[R], and \f[V]YT\f[R] tags are not part of the SAMv1 standard
but are widely used and originate from bowtie2 and usearch.
.SS Unmapped queries
.PP
By default, a query that produces no hit does not generate a SAM record.
When \f[V]--output_no_hits\f[R] is given, vsearch emits a single record
for each such query with \f[V]FLAG\f[R] set to \f[V]4\f[R] (unmapped),
\f[V]RNAME\f[R] set to \f[V]*\f[R], \f[V]POS\f[R] set to \f[V]0\f[R],
\f[V]CIGAR\f[R] set to \f[V]*\f[R], and no optional tags.
The \f[V]SEQ\f[R] field contains the original query sequence.
.SS Character set and line endings
.PP
vsearch writes ASCII characters only.
Lines are terminated by a single line feed character (ASCII 10); no
carriage return is emitted.
Fields and tags are separated by a single tab character (ASCII 9).
vsearch does not pad fields and does not emit trailing tabs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
A minimal SAM file with header, one forward-strand hit, one
reverse-strand hit for a second query, and one unmapped query
(\f[V]--output_no_hits\f[R] in effect):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]HD VN:1.0  SO:unsorted GO:query
\[at]SQ SN:ref1 LN:10   M5:0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef UR:file:refs.fasta
\[at]SQ SN:ref2 LN:11   M5:fedcba9876543210fedcba9876543210 UR:file:refs.fasta
\[at]PG ID:vsearch  VN:2.30.6   CL:vsearch --usearch_global queries.fasta --db refs.fasta --id 0.9 --samout out.sam --samheader
q1  0   ref1    1   255 10M *   0   0   ACGTACGTAC  *   AS:i:100    XN:i:0  XM:i:0  XO:i:0  XG:i:0  NM:i:0  MD:Z:10 YT:Z:UU
q2  16  ref2    1   255 5M1I6M  *   0   0   ACGTAGCGTACG    *   AS:i:92 XN:i:0  XM:i:0  XO:i:1  XG:i:1  NM:i:1  MD:Z:11 YT:Z:UU
q3  4   *   0   255 *   *   0   0   TTTTTTTT    *
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The second record has the reverse-strand bit \f[V]0x010\f[R] (decimal
\f[V]16\f[R]) set, meaning that \f[V]SEQ\f[R] is the reverse complement
of the original query and the alignment lies on the reverse strand of
\f[V]ref2\f[R].
The third record is unmapped.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
